Why mob (not the newest tag, release_0_9_27 / 2017-12-12): release_0_9_27
DOES carry a complete aarch64 backend (arm64-gen.c/arm64-link.c) but its
include/stdarg.h predates modern musl headers — Alpine's current musl
declares libc `va_list` via a bare `__builtin_va_list` token
(bits/alltypes.h, `__NEED_va_list` block) that release_0_9_27's parser
does not accept, so `tcc -c` on any TU that includes <stdio.h> fails with
"';' expected (got "va_list")" before reaching codegen. The mob branch's
include/stdarg.h (see that file) resolves va_start/va_arg/va_copy/va_end
through tcc's builtin_va_* forms compatible with modern musl's
`__builtin_va_list`-based headers — verified empirically (see
test/run_tcc_hello.sh): mob-branch tcc compiles and runs hello.c against
Alpine's live musl headers; release_0_9_27 does not. This was found by
direct build+test in the target arm64 musl Alpine container, not assumed.

The mob build also fails to build lib/libtcc1.a's OPTIONAL bounds-checker
object (bcheck.o -> wchar_t redefinition against Alpine's current
stddef.h) — this is a separate, non-essential debug-instrumentation
library asset; `make`'s failure on that target does not stop `tcc` itself
from building or the compile+link+run proof from passing. Left as a known
gap (informational only) rather than patched, since patching upstream
source is out of scope for this vendoring bead; a later S2 bead can pin
forward or patch if bounds-checking is ever needed.

